  "generated_text": "We are given the equations $\\log (xy^3)= 1$ and $\\log (x^2y) = 1$.\n\nUsing the properties of logarithms, we can rewrite these equations as:\n\n$\\log x + 3\\log y = 1$ and $2\\log x + \\log y = 1$.\n\nWe can solve this system of equations by subtracting the second equation from the first equation:\n\n$2\\log y = 0 \\Rightarrow \\log y = 0$.\n\nSubstituting $\\log y = 0$ into the second equation, we get:\n\n$2\\log x = 1 \\Rightarrow \\log x = \\frac{1}{2}$.\n\nNow, we can find $\\log (xy)$ by adding $\\log x$ and $\\log y$:\n\n$\\log (xy) = \\log x + \\log y = \\frac{1}{2} + 0 = \\boxed{\\frac{1}{2}}$.\nThe answer is: \\frac{1}{2}",
